{
  "_meta": {
    "title": "English conjunctions",
    "language": "en",
    "description": "The closed class of English conjunctions: coordinating (FANBOYS), correlative pairs, subordinating (grouped by the clause relation they mark), and conjunctive adverbs (which connect but are punctuated with a semicolon and are technically adverbs). Many forms are multi-category \u2014 as/since/while/before/after/than are conjunctions AND prepositions or adverbs depending on use; the `also` field records that, and a consumer must disambiguate by position, not by the word.",
    "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
    "credits": "../CREDITS.md",
    "source_notes": "The four-way split (coordinating / correlative / subordinating / conjunctive-adverb) and the subordinating functional groups (time, cause, condition, contrast, comparison, place, manner, purpose) are standard descriptive English grammar, not a novel classification. Individual conjunctions are facts about the language.",
    "status": "seed \u2014 a broad but not exhaustive list, ASSISTANT-DRAFTED and unreviewed; verify categories/multi-category flags before teaching from it.",
    "method": "assistant",
    "reviewed": false
  },
